  "kind": "how-rainbows-form",
  "apiVersion": 1,
  "releaseId": "how-rainbows-form-v2",
  "description": "How a rainbow forms, for a child of about eight. Outdoor model: a low Sun, water drops (a rain shower or a fountain) and an explorer called Mia. A primary rainbow is every direction 42 degrees from the point opposite the Sun; Mia sees it only where sunlit drops lie in those directions and she is looking that way. rainbowStatus says why she does or does not see one. The rainbow is drawn only in Mia’s window, never in the meadow, because it is light reaching her eyes and not an object. Close-up model: one drop as a flat diagram; light bends in, bounces off the back and bends out on the Sun’s side, each colour by a slightly different amount. Sizes, distances and the width of the colour fan are illustrative. Not modelled: the second bow, brightness, drop size, fog bows.",
